<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure" target="china_tech_trade_rivalry::export_control_strength" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Escalating sanctions regimes around the Russia war expand regulatory capacity and legal precedents for controlling dual‑use goods, prompting governments to quickly broaden and tighten export controls that are then applied to high‑tech transfers to China.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure" target="china_tech_trade_rivalry::foreign_capital_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising sanctions and the attendant risk of secondary measures increase counterparty and regulatory risk perceptions, causing international investors and banks to scale back capital flows into firms and sectors perceived as exposed to geopolitical spillovers (including Chinese tech firms).</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure" target="europe_political_turbule::domestic_market_volatili" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Tightened sanctions on Russia reduce energy and trade flows and raise uncertainty, producing immediate energy-price shocks and balance-sheet repricing that increase short-term volatility in European financial markets.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">europe_political_turbule</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure" target="iran_hormuz_escalation::oil_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Escalating sanctions on Russian energy and financial sectors reduce Russian export capacity and/or push buyers to shun Russian crude, tightening global supply and putting upward pressure on benchmark oil prices.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure" target="iran_hormuz_escalation::maritime_security_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Greater sanctions on Russia incentivize deeper Russia–Iran security and arms cooperation (transfers, tactics, intelligence sharing), which increases the capability and coordination of disruptive operations against commercial shipping in the Gulf, elevating maritime security risk.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure" target="united_states_realignmen::judicial_challenge_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Broadening or intensifying sanctions creates legal exposure for affected firms, intermediaries and third‑country actors, prompting more lawsuits, injunctions and administrative challenges to executive sanctions actions.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">united_states_realignmen</data>
</edge>
